# Line endings get one answer here, not one per contributor's core.autocrlf.
#
# WHY this file exists. docs/scenario-map.md is generated by
# tools/gen_scenario_map.py and then checked byte-for-byte by CI
# (`git diff --exit-code -- docs/scenario-map.md`). With no .gitattributes the
# committed bytes depended on whoever ran the generator: a Windows contributor
# with core.autocrlf=true happened to be fine, one with autocrlf=false or
# `input` committed CRLF, and the Linux CI job then regenerated LF and reported
# all ~98 lines as changed with a message telling them to run the generator and
# commit the result — which is exactly what they had just done. Nothing in the
# repository decided this; each machine's git config did.
#
# `text=auto` makes git normalise every detected-text blob to LF *in the
# repository* on commit, whatever the working tree holds. `eol=lf` goes further
# and checks those files out as LF on all three operating systems, so a checkout
# on Windows is byte-identical to one on macOS or Linux — which is the property
# the CI byte-comparison actually needs, rather than "happens to agree because
# of how each contributor configured git".
#
# `eol=lf` is only safe because tools/gen_scenario_map.py now pins
# `newline=""` on its write. Before that fix it emitted CRLF on Windows, and
# under `eol=lf` every local regeneration would have shown as a whole-file
# diff — punishing a Windows contributor for a bug in the generator. The two
# changes belong together; do not revert one without the other.
*                text=auto eol=lf

# Windows shells genuinely need CRLF in the working tree: cmd.exe mis-parses an
# LF-only .bat (it reads past the line end and can execute a truncated
# command), and docs/OPERATIONS.md documents cmd.exe as an operator shell.
# These come after the `*` line on purpose — for a given attribute the last
# matching pattern wins, so this overrides the eol=lf above.
*.bat            text eol=crlf
*.cmd            text eol=crlf
*.ps1            text eol=crlf

# LF, pinned explicitly, for the files something *executes* rather than reads.
# These already inherit eol=lf from the `*` line, so today these rules change
# nothing — that is the point. They exist so the guarantee survives an edit to
# the catch-all, and so nobody extends the CRLF block above by symmetry ("we did
# .ps1, so .sh too"). A CRLF shell script fails in a way that reads as anything
# but a line-ending problem: `/bin/sh^M: bad interpreter` on a Linux container,
# or a value that silently carries a trailing CR. Concretely here:
#   *.sh/*.bash — no shell script is tracked today; when one arrives it will be
#     run by /bin/sh in the python:3.12-slim image or by a researcher's bash.
#   Dockerfile  — ./Dockerfile and agents/Dockerfile. A CR at the end of a
#     `RUN pip install ... \` continuation is not a line continuation, so the
#     build breaks or, worse, installs a package name ending in CR.
#   .env*       — .env.example is copied to .env (README quick start). The app
#     reads it through python-dotenv, which strips the CR; `docker run
#     --env-file` and `set -a; . .env` do not, and both are ordinary operator
#     moves. The value at risk is the Cornell LiteLLM virtual key, and a key
#     with a trailing CR fails as "invalid credentials", not as "bad newline".
*.sh             text eol=lf
*.bash           text eol=lf
Dockerfile       text eol=lf
Dockerfile.*     text eol=lf
*.Dockerfile     text eol=lf
.env*            text eol=lf

# Binary: never newline-convert, never diff as text. Without these, git's
# content heuristic decides per file, and a .wav or .zip that happens to look
# textual in its first 8000 bytes gets corrupted on a Windows checkout.
#
# This is not hypothetical in this repository. The three tracked PDFs were
# committed before this file existed, under core.autocrlf=true, and every
# checkout under that setting inserted a CR before each of their 118/143/181 LF
# bytes. The stored blobs are intact — nothing was lost from history — but the
# file on disk is not: the inserted bytes shift everything after the first one,
# so `startxref` still names offset 9429 while the xref table has moved there
# and back. A strict reader calls the file damaged, a lenient one silently
# rebuilds the index, and git never said a word either way, because with `text`
# set both directions of the conversion are "correct". `*.pdf binary` is what
# makes a checkout produce the intact blob rather than the shifted copy
# (verified below).
#
# Media types the study itself produces are listed even though .gitignore keeps
# them out of git today (`data/`, `*.wav`): the point of the list is that if one
# is ever force-added — as reddit-analysis/data/processed/*.json already was,
# despite `data/` — it is not silently newline-mangled on the way in.
*.png            binary
*.jpg            binary
*.jpeg           binary
*.gif            binary
*.pdf            binary
*.zip            binary
*.gz             binary
*.wav            binary
*.webm           binary
*.mp4            binary
*.ico            binary
*.woff           binary
*.woff2          binary

# Coverage note — checked against the tree at 96f0372, not assumed.
#
# Every tracked extension is: .py .yaml .yml .md .json .csv .txt .toml .tf
# .tfvars .hcl .js .html .example .gitkeep .gitignore .gitattributes .dockerignore
# and the two extensionless Dockerfiles — all text, all covered by the `*` rule.
# The tracked non-text files are 10 .png, 3 .pdf and an empty `.zip`, each
# covered by a `binary` line above (`*` in a gitattributes pattern matches the
# empty string, so `*.zip` does match a file literally named `.zip`).
#
# Verified by materialising all 183 tracked paths into an empty directory and
# comparing byte for byte, which is the only check that sees past a stale
# working tree:
#   * `git ls-files --eol` in the index: 157 lf, 16 none, 10 -text. No blob is
#     crlf or mixed, so the `git add --renormalize .` that this file used to ask
#     for is a confirmed no-op — the history is already clean and there is
#     nothing here for someone with commit rights to do.
#   * In the fresh checkout: 14 binary paths byte-identical to their blobs, 169
#     text paths with no CRLF, zero violations, and all three PDFs parse
#     (header, %%EOF, startxref landing on `xref`).
# tests/test_deploy_portability.py re-runs both of those, so this note is a
# statement the suite keeps true rather than a claim that ages.
#
# STALE WORKING TREES — read this before trusting a binary in an old clone.
# A clone made before this file landed still holds the CRLF-mangled PDFs, and
# `git status` calls it clean: the index stat cache still matches the size
# recorded at that old checkout, so git never re-hashes them. Nothing detects
# this from inside such a tree, and the tests above cannot either, because they
# deliberately compare a fresh checkout rather than the local files.
# Re-cloning is the safe fix. In place, with a CLEAN tree and no work you mind
# losing — this discards every uncommitted change:
#     git rm --cached -r . -q && git reset --hard
